// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: properties, purity, soundness, computational-properties
// @expected-error: E503
// @expected-error-count: 4
//
// Pin (T0791) — the purity gate must see an impure expression through any
// wrapper, not only through the wrappers someone remembered to list.
//
// Property inference declared 45 of the language’s 73 expression forms pure
// outright — without looking at what they contained.
// The comment on that arm read "Default: pure for unhandled cases", and that
// is what it did.  So ONE PAIR OF PARENTHESES defeated the gate:
//
//     pure fn s() -> Int { spawn { 1 }; 2 }        error<E503>
//     pure fn s() -> Int { (spawn { 1 }); 2 }      accepted, silently
//
// A `pure fn` is a compiler-verified promise of no side effects, and the
// compiler was verifying a promise it had stopped reading.
//
// What kept this invisible for so long is that the 28 forms which WERE listed
// are the ones people write: `let _ = spawn { 1 }` reported correctly, so the
// gate looked like it worked, and every ordinary test of it passed.
//
// `spawn` is the impure operation used here because it is the one that
// `validate_for_pure_fn` rejects with no room for argument.  Fallible and
// Divergent are deliberately ALLOWED in a pure function — errors and panics
// are deterministic — so a predicate built on `?` or `panic` would pin
// nothing.
//
// Its control lives in `purity_allows_pure_wrappers.vr`: the same four
// wrapper shapes around genuinely pure expressions must stay silent.  Union
// of children can only ADD properties, so the failure mode of this fix is a
// pure function that stops compiling, and that file is what catches it.

pure fn parenthesised() -> Int {
    (spawn { 1 });
    2
}

pure fn behind_a_projection() -> Int {
    let _ = (spawn { 1 }, 0).1;
    2
}

pure fn inside_an_interpolation() -> Int {
    let _ = f"{spawn { 1 }}";
    2
}

pure fn bare_for_comparison() -> Int {
    spawn { 1 };
    2
}

fn main() {
    print(parenthesised() + behind_a_projection() + inside_an_interpolation()
        + bare_for_comparison());
}
